<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constantine II of Greece</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1940">1940</a>–<a href="/years/2023">2023</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Constantine II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> was the last king of Greece, reigning from 1964. He clashed with the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgios-papandreou">Georgios Papandreou</a> and accepted the colonels' coup of April 1967, but his failed counter-coup that December forced him into exile. The junta abolished the monarchy in 1973, and a referendum in 1974 confirmed the republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constantine-ii-of-greece.jpg" alt="Constantine II of Greece" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Allan warren; derivative work by Daffy123 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Constantine.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Constantine was born on 2 June 1940 in Psychiko, a suburb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, the only son of Crown Prince Paul and Crown Princess Frederica. His father was the younger brother and heir of King George II. Italy invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> in October 1940 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in April 1941, and the infant Constantine was evacuated with his mother and sister to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> and then, on 30 April 1941, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The family later found refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. They returned to Greece in 1946, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-civil-war">Greek Civil War</a>, and when George II died on 1 April 1947, Paul became king and Constantine crown prince at the age of six.</p><p>He was a keen sailor. At the 1960 Rome Olympics he was helmsman of the yacht Nireus, which won the gold medal in the Dragon class with Odysseus Eskitzoglou and Georgios Zaimis, the first Greek gold medal since 1912.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Paul died of cancer on 6 March 1964, and Constantine, aged 23, succeeded him. Later that year he married Princess Anne-Marie of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. His reign was marked by political instability. In the Apostasia of 1965, the king refused the prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgios-papandreou">Georgios Papandreou</a>'s wish to take over the defence ministry after the ASPIDA affair, and Papandreou resigned on 15 July; at least 39 members of parliament then left his Centre Union. Historians suspect the king and his mother were interested in a coup from mid-1965, though the royalist C. M. Woodhouse rejected such evidence as certainly untrue.</p><p>The Colonels' Coup of 21 April 1967 left Constantine with no loyal forces to rely on, and he reluctantly agreed to swear in the junta on condition that it include mostly civilian ministers. He disliked the regime from the outset and planned a countercoup, which failed. On 13 December 1967 he fled the country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He remained head of state in exile until the junta abolished the monarchy on 1 June 1973, a decision confirmed by a referendum in July that he contested. After the junta fell, a second referendum in December 1974, which he was not allowed to return to campaign for, confirmed the abolition by a majority of almost 70 per cent. He publicly accepted the verdict. Archives published later show that between 1975 and 1978 he was involved in a conspiracy against the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstantinos-karamanlis">Konstantinos Karamanlis</a>, which never materialised.</p><p>He lived for decades in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> only briefly, as for his mother's funeral in 1981, which he was allowed to attend for six hours. A dispute over royal property ended in 2002, when the European Court of Human Rights awarded his family €13.7 million. In 2013 he moved back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, where he died on 10 January 2023 after a stroke, aged 82.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Constantine II of Greece” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1946 the restored monarchy ruled through conservative governments under Alexandros Papagos and Konstantinos Karamanlis, with left-wing parties restricted, until the crisis of 1965 between King <b>Constantine II</b> and George Papandreou.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In Greece the clash between <b>Constantine II of Greece</b> and the prime minister George Papandreou in 1965 opened a period of instability that ended in the colonels’ coup of 1967, and in 1974 Greeks voted to abolish the monarchy.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Weaknesses</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Psychiko Palace, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Hygeia Hospital, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.01,23.77&amp;year=1993" aria-label="Psychiko Palace on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5142 798 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Constantine II of Greece"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5592 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5592 1068h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.01,23.77&amp;year=1993">What was here in 1993? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
